{"item_id":"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151286","version_id":"a393edb1af792a0f22f58a9c9be7640474dbeb4ed41d7ec336f5428b607cf2bc","decision":"defer","reason":"This is fresh and highly relevant CAD/PLM coverage, but it is an Autodesk executive interview about features expected in future quarters rather than a check of released capability. The productivity, adoption, and agentic-PLM claims are vendor claims with no disclosed tasks, accuracy, failure cases, or independent evaluation. A released workflow and reproducible evidence of how it extracts and validates PLM information would make it checkable.","piece_ids":[]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151329","version_id":"a5a6e64fd4f2a87b5b45be5afd0c01d19fd3ee1e8b8f12be9e7e49964e03d82b","decision":"defer","reason":"The item is fresh and relevant to CAD-to-simulation workflows, and it clearly distinguishes Comsol's upcoming release from a released product. However, the SimScale agent's claim that it can repair simulation-readiness, choose setups, mesh, run physics, and report results comes from SimScale's press release, with no disclosed cases or verification of result quality. The Autodesk material repeats the same underlying AU announcement covered elsewhere and is not independent corroboration.","piece_ids":[]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151563","version_id":"90e3adc0a87a45dc20689ae62db7125fe378f8191522f5954fae4ace5e96c803","decision":"accept","reason":"This is fresh, directly relevant CAD evidence with a named practitioner test and checkable outcomes: the author imported Backflip STEP outputs into Fusion, applied the specified material, and verified exact mass for two TooTallToby drawing challenges. It also records meaningful failure conditions: a Fusion feature-tree import was incomplete and the system produced only one of seven bodies on a multibody drawing. The tests establish success on two constrained benchmark-style drawings, not general accuracy, manufacturability, or reliable reconstruction from photos and video.","piece_ids":["01","02","03"]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.onshape.com/en/blog/ai-generated-thumbnail-description-search","version_id":"b2755a997c3cdda71421bbdd40ee9721d353cbc20ed2f1f80e91169757b9bbd2","decision":"accept","reason":"This fresh first-party announcement documents a currently testable, opt-in Onshape Labs feature: AI-generated descriptions of static model thumbnails are searchable for public documents only. The publisher explicitly limits the scope and labels internal-data, supplier-catalog, reuse, and cost-reduction scenarios as speculative, which keeps the claim appropriately bounded. It is useful commercial perspective, though evidence from working engineers and private datasets remains missing.","piece_ids":["01","03"]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.onshape.com/en/blog/featurescript-mcp-server-use-cases-text-code-cad","version_id":"9656a0a35f95ac0e0adb6ba1530e2759342633cbcecba8de9948699a032ab5a8","decision":"defer","reason":"The FeatureScript MCP Server is relevant to text-to-code-to-CAD, but this vendor use-case article provides illustrative scenarios rather than observed outcomes. Claims that an LLM can preserve parametric intent, manufacturing accuracy, optimize code, or integrate analysis have no stated model, prompts, test models, graders, failure rate, or independent check. Reproducible examples showing generated FeatureScript passing geometry and standards checks would settle the central claims.","piece_ids":[]}
{"item_id":"podcast:173f6c2b-e1b8-4de0-b7b7-4b15658d52e6","version_id":"da6be0ed10587e503de7706e1ad94cd3d0409d0c4664a004a631d6f27c500a73","decision":"reject","reason":"Although fresh and timestamped, this episode concerns narrative intelligence, coaching, and human identity rather than 3D modeling, CAD, geometry, simulation-ready assets, or physical-system verification. It supplies personal perspectives rather than checkable technical evidence relevant to the publication's audience. It therefore fails the relevance criterion.","piece_ids":[]}
{"item_id":"podcast:509842f7-f592-432c-b01a-ad26a2ae1e31","version_id":"122e3cc3de2a41fbd9f0de08a089614db628a34697f00a720e1d3abf5b405dca","decision":"reject","reason":"This fresh, timestamped discussion is about general education in an AI-driven world, not frontier 3D modeling, CAD, or physical AI. Its brief reference to mechanical engineering does not provide a capability, geometry, simulation, or verification claim that would help technical product managers make build decisions. It fails the relevance criterion.","piece_ids":[]}
{"item_id":"podcast:b836fa1b-9246-43d5-8cbb-cf1446760a6d","version_id":"a0beee0f49195b6f0b08b75af793b6f6acaf56a159b58771b1c5d8f6c294b282","decision":"defer","reason":"The episode is fresh and relevant robotics context because it discusses world simulation and the different sensor constraints of physical systems. But it is an NVIDIA executive interview, and the sampled automatic transcript supplies no checkable experiment, deployment result, disclosed evaluation conditions, or independently verified claim. Audio-confirmed passages plus released model, data, and transfer evaluation details would be needed before accepting it.","piece_ids":[]}
{"item_id":"podcast:https://physicalaipodcast.co/episodes/008","version_id":"1cb4eefeb597341d29e650ad7c122c9eea10b1aa146aef9c4a6214acdf2c2983","decision":"defer","reason":"This is fresh and promising robotics context for piece 2 because it explicitly distinguishes simulation ranking from real-arm evidence and reports a purported blind evaluation with stated task and attempt counts. However, the numerical and attributional details appear only in an automatic transcript and must be checked against the audio and underlying Black Forest Labs, Positronic Robotics, and leaderboard materials. The reported external test was commissioned by the model developer, so even if confirmed it is not independent corroboration of broad deployment performance.","piece_ids":[]}
{"item_id":"podcast:https://physicalaipodcast.co/episodes/009","version_id":"13aaf6bfc525bce0b9a1887162b0c0c3d4dd024b9bafd1c223a5bc48bd063692","decision":"defer","reason":"This is fresh, relevant supporting robotics coverage that frames generalization and autonomy in terms of held-out tasks, interventions, simulation, and a real-robot result. Yet the reported 70%, 6%, and 10% results, as well as the code-and-weights claim, are taken from an automatic transcript and require confirmation against the audio and cited Tsinghua paper and repository. Even as described, most evidence is simulation and the physical result is one task, so it does not establish general physical transfer.","piece_ids":[]}
